{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "001R", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$x \\times y$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0003": "$$\nh_{x \\times y}(w) = h_x(w) \\times h_y(w)\n$$", "MATH_0004": "$w$", "MATH_0005": "$x \\times y$", "MATH_0006": "$w \\mapsto h_x(w) \\times h_y(w)$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:ae9b5e8cace767f476810826d0b7ba0f34a70defe5a459af45f718af91f79780", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:f291ffa2f1a92242d6b102cd3949f7c9ae2e1cbc34808305f13935c4bf1c4d00", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIf a product exists it is unique up to unique\nisomorphism. This follows from the Yoneda lemma as\nthe definition requires <MATH_0001> to be an object\nof <MATH_0002> such that\n<MATH_0003>\nfunctorially in <MATH_0004>. In other words the product <MATH_0005>\nis an object representing the functor\n<MATH_0006>.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f4c1d1af773e785b49a4c00df6bedfc7ac42e4271c32b649220eda3d9decdc6d", "unit_id": "tag:001R:prose-0001"}
